===== execution reference =====
# Execution protocol

Read this once before iteration 1, and re-read it whenever you are unsure what to
do mid-loop. Uncertainty mid-loop is a signal to consult the files, not to
improvise — improvisation is how loops drift.

## State schema: status and stop_reason

Exits come in three kinds — **converged** (success with evidence),
**ceilings** (iteration budget, wall clock, iteration timeout, turn cap), and
**escape hatches** (`blocked`, `stalled`, `paused`, `aborted`). The tables
below are the complete exit set: if the loop is stopping for a reason not
listed, pick the nearest hatch and explain in the journal — never invent a
new status.

Canonical status set — use these exact strings everywhere (state.json, LOOP
STATUS lines, /goal conditions, driver, reports):

`running | converged | paused | blocked | stalled | aborted | max_iterations`

Every non-`running` status carries a machine-readable `stop_reason`
(`running` → `null`):

| stop_reason | typical status | meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `criterion_met` | converged | all completion criteria passed with evidence |
| `iteration_budget_exhausted` | max_iterations | hit `max_iterations` |
| `max_wall_time` | aborted | driver wall-clock ceiling reached |
| `stalled_no_metric_improvement` | stalled | `stall_threshold` hit, stall policy exhausted |
| `stalled_no_state_update` | blocked | agent launches not advancing `iteration` (driver-detected) |
| `blocked_permission_required` | blocked | needs out-of-scope change / disallowed tool / scope violation |
| `blocked_verification_unavailable` | blocked | verification command itself errors repeatedly |
| `blocked_external_dependency` | blocked | blocked on something outside the repo |
| `blocked_criterion_doubt` | blocked | a criterion appears wrong or unachievable |
| `blocked_checkpoint_failed` | blocked | git/file checkpointing itself failed |
| `user_pause_required` | paused | human checkpoint due per plan |
| `manual_abort` | aborted | user said stop |
| `aborted_agent_error` | aborted | agent process failing hard, no cleaner category |

`state.json` full schema (all writes atomic — tmp file, then rename):

```json
{"status": "running", "stop_reason": null,
 "iteration": 0, "max_iterations": 10, "stall_threshold": 3,
 "best_metric": null, "iterations_since_improvement": 0,
 "git": {"origin_branch": "main", "origin_head": "<sha>",
          "loop_branch": "loop/<slug>"},
 "started_at": "<ISO8601>", "updated_at": "<ISO8601>"}
```

Structured per-iteration records live in `.loop/iterations/<NNNN>.json`
(schema in the iteration prompt): `journal.md` is for humans; the records are
what lets the next fresh-context agent — or the final report — recover state
without parsing prose.

`.loop/backlog.json` (optional — multi-item goals, seeded in Phase 2) is a
JSON array:

```json
[{"id": "B1", "title": "tests/api all green", "passes": false,
  "priority": 1, "source": "plan", "evidence": null}]
```

`id`/`title`/`passes` are required; `priority` (1 = first), `source`
(`plan` | `discovered`), `evidence` (one line: iteration + command that
proved it), and optional `check` (a command that proves this item on its own)
keep the audit trail. Append-only discipline: add items, flip `passes` with
printed evidence, never delete or reword. Note the all-passes checker counts
flags the worker itself flipped — bookkeeping, not proof — which is why the
plan must AND it with at least one ungameable command criterion (or use
per-item `check` commands). `.loop/LESSONS.md` is
its prose sibling: an append-only list of one-line signs
(`- SIGN (iter 3): <rule>`) that every later iteration reads and obeys —
the lessons that would otherwise scroll out of the journal tail.

## Engines: who starts the next iteration

The single most common way loops fail is that nothing restarts them: the model
finishes one pass, summarizes, and ends its turn. Each engine solves this
differently. Know which engine the plan specifies and follow its section exactly.
Every plan carries a **Crank line** (plan §9) naming who starts each next
iteration, what must stay true, and the kill switch — quote it when reporting.

### Engine B — external driver script (the unattended default)

Mechanics: `.loop/run_loop.sh` (instantiated in Phase 2 from
`assets/run-loop-template.sh`) loops while `state.json` says `status ==
"running"` and ceilings are unbreached, invoking `AGENT_CMD_JSON` once per
iteration with the fixed prompt in `.loop/ITERATION_PROMPT.md`. Each invocation is a
**fresh context** — the Ralph insight: intelligence comes from the spec, the
verifiable checks, and the on-disk state, not from one long session. Under
`nohup`/`tmux` it keeps cranking with the terminal closed and the human asleep;
this is why unattended intent defaults here.

Crank line: *the driver process starts every next iteration; it keeps cranking
while the process lives and `state.json` says `running`; kill switch:
`touch .loop/STOP`, Ctrl+C / kill the process, or set `status` in
`state.json`.*

Consequences you must respect as the worker model (when you are the invoked
iteration):

1. You remember nothing. Step 1 of the checklist (read plan, state, journal
   tail) is your entire memory. Trust files over any impression of familiarity.
2. Do exactly one iteration, print the `LOOP STATUS:` line, and stop. Never
   start a second iteration — the driver owns continuation, and double
   iterations corrupt the state accounting.
3. To stop the loop for any reason, set `state.json` `status` accordingly; the
   driver reads it before every launch. That is *your* only channel to the
   driver — `.loop/STOP` is the operator's.
4. Leave the working tree clean (checkpoint per plan) — the next iteration
   starts from what you leave behind.

Driver guarantees (built into the template):

- **Single instance**: a portable `mkdir` lock (`.loop/driver.lockdir`)
  refuses a second concurrent driver; stale locks after a crash are removed
  manually (`rmdir .loop/driver.lockdir`).
- **No lying state**: every driver-side terminal decision (budget, wall
  clock, dead-agent guard, scope violation) is written back to `state.json`
  atomically with a `stop_reason` — the file never ends a run still saying
  `running`.
- **Real exit codes**: the agent runs via a JSON-argv subprocess wrapper
  (`AGENT_CMD_JSON`, `PROMPT_MODE=arg|stdin`) and the driver captures the
  agent's exit code, not `tee`'s.
- **Scope enforcement outside the model**: after each iteration the driver
  runs `.loop/check_scope.py` (if present); a violation while status is still
  `running` sets `blocked` / `blocked_permission_required`.
- **Wall clock is opt-in**: `MAX_WALL_MINUTES=0` (default) disables it; a
  non-zero value must mirror plan §6 `max_wall_time` — the driver never
  imposes a ceiling the plan doesn't state.
- **Hang guard**: `ITERATION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES` (0 = disabled; mirrors plan §6
  `iteration_timeout`) kills an agent run that exceeds it (process-group
  SIGTERM→SIGKILL, exit code 124) — a stuck headless permission prompt or
  network call cannot silently end an overnight run; the dead-agent guard
  then counts it as a failed launch.
- **Pilot mode**: `MAX_LAUNCHES=N` stops the driver after N launches, state
  untouched. `MAX_LAUNCHES=1 bash .loop/run_loop.sh` is the mandatory first
  run: watch one iteration write its record, append the journal, and commit —
  auth, permission-allowlist, and prompt bugs surface here, not at 3 a.m.
- **Kill-switch file**: `touch .loop/STOP` — the driver aborts
  (`aborted`/`manual_abort`) before the next launch and removes the file.
  Easier than editing JSON, works from any shell, and the worker honors the
  same file at the start of its next iteration (checklist Step 1) — which
  also makes STOP work under Engines A and C.
- **Final report**: on termination the driver writes `.loop/FINAL_REPORT.md`
  from `state.json` + `iterations/*.json` (status, stop_reason, criteria/
  metric trajectory, files touched). For the full human-facing Phase 4 report,
  re-invoke the skill in an interactive session — it will read the same files.

Operator notes (tell the user in Phase 2): **pilot first** —
`MAX_LAUNCHES=1 bash .loop/run_loop.sh` and watch one full iteration succeed;
then `bash .loop/run_loop.sh` (foreground) or `nohup bash .loop/run_loop.sh &`
/ inside `tmux` for walk-away runs; rerunning the script later resumes from
`state.json`. Headless agent invocations answer no interactive permission
prompts — a tool call that would prompt simply fails — so grant the minimal
allowlist the plan's verification and checkpoint commands need (e.g. Claude
Code `--allowedTools "Bash(pytest:*) Bash(git:*) Edit Write Read"`); that
argues for tighter tool permissions and scope in the plan, and ideally a
sandbox or dedicated branch/worktree. A wrong allowlist is exactly what the
pilot run exists to catch. Rerunning resumes only while state says `running`;
after a `paused`/`blocked` stop, first set `status` back to `"running"` and
`stop_reason` to `null`, then rerun. Mid-run controls: `touch .loop/STOP`
aborts cleanly; writing `.loop/STEER.md` hands the next iteration a one-shot
directive (read → journaled → deleted).

### Engine A — Claude Code `/goal` (attended hands-free; not a background job)

**Hard prerequisites — confirm all of these with the user before handing over
the command; if any fails, or the real intent is unattended, recommend Engine B
instead:**

1. Claude Code ≥ v2.1.139, workspace trust dialog accepted, hooks enabled.
2. **Auto mode ON** (tool calls auto-approved). Without it the loop halts at
   the first permission prompt. This is a hard precondition, not an
   optimization.
3. **The session stays open for the whole run.** `/goal` advances **exactly one
   turn per Stop-hook evaluation**, and only in a live session. It is not a
   scheduler, not a daemon, not background work. Closing the client, sleeping
   the laptop, or losing the session stops the crank (a still-active goal is
   restored on `--resume`, with counters reset).

Crank line: *the /goal Stop-hook evaluator starts every next turn; it keeps
cranking only while this client is open, the goal is active, and Auto mode is
on; kill switch: `/goal clear` (interactive) or Ctrl+C (headless `claude -p`).*

Mechanics: the user runs `/goal <condition>`. Setting the goal starts a turn
immediately with the condition as the directive. After every turn, an
independent small evaluator model reads the **conversation transcript** (it
cannot run tools or read files) and answers yes/no; if the loop should
continue, a new turn starts with the evaluator's reason as the next
instruction. Headless variant: `claude -p "/goal <condition>"` runs the loop in
one foreground process.

Consequences you must respect as the worker model:

1. **One iteration per turn.** Run the full per-iteration checklist inside the
   turn, then end the turn. Ending the turn is safe and required — the
   machinery restarts it.
2. **The evaluator sees only what you print.** Verification output must appear
   in the conversation, not just in the journal. A criterion whose evidence was
   never printed is, to the evaluator, unmet.
3. **Never ask the user questions mid-loop** — a question doesn't pause
   anything; the evaluator just restarts you. Route everything through the
   `LOOP STATUS:` line.
4. End **every** turn with exactly one line in this format:
   `LOOP STATUS: running — iteration 3 recorded, criterion C1 still failing`
   `LOOP STATUS: paused — human checkpoint due per plan §8`
   Allowed statuses: `running | converged | paused | blocked | stalled |
   aborted | max_iterations`.

**Honest failure modes — state these to the user up front:**

- `paused`/`blocked`/`stalled` stop the loop **only if the evaluator honors the
  STOP clause in the condition**. The evaluator is a small model; if the
  condition doesn't spell out that these statuses mean STOP, its literal
  reading of "is the goal met?" is "not met → continue", and the loop is
  trapped restarting a worker that is trying to stop. Even with the clause,
  treat stopping as *likely, not guaranteed* — if the loop keeps restarting
  after a `paused`/`blocked` status, the human must run `/goal clear`.
- There is no built-in token budget; the turn cap inside the condition is the
  only ceiling the evaluator enforces.

Composing the goal condition (done in Phase 2; stored in plan §9). **Frame the
question the evaluator answers as "should the loop STOP", never as "is the
work converged."** Rules:

- The evaluator cannot read `.loop/LOOP_PLAN.md`, so **the criteria must be
  spelled out verbatim inside the condition text**. The plan file is for the
  worker; the condition is for the judge.
- Enumerate STOP causes explicitly — success is only one of them. Escape
  statuses and the turn cap are the others, and each must be labeled as a
  STOP-the-loop answer, not left for the evaluator to infer.
- Keep it under 4,000 characters. One goal per session; compound unrelated
  objectives overwhelm the evaluator — split into sequential goals.
- Sanity-check from the evaluator's perspective before handing it over: "if
  the worker's last message says `LOOP STATUS: blocked`, does this text
  unambiguously tell me to answer YES (stop)?"

Template:

```
Directive for the worker: work per the approved plan in .loop/LOOP_PLAN.md —
one loop iteration per turn, update .loop/state.json and append to
.loop/journal.md every turn, print all verification output in the
conversation, never ask the user questions, and end every turn with a
"LOOP STATUS:" line.

Condition for the evaluator: answer YES (goal satisfied — STOP the loop) if
ANY of the following holds:
(a) ALL of these criteria pass, with their verification output printed in the
conversation this session: <criterion 1: command + expected observable
result>; <criterion 2: …>;
(b) the assistant's latest message contains a line starting with
"LOOP STATUS:" followed by converged, paused, blocked, stalled, aborted, or
max_iterations — treat paused, blocked, stalled, aborted, and max_iterations
as STOP-the-loop signals, NOT as "not yet done";
(c) <N> turns have elapsed since this goal was set.
Otherwise answer NO (the loop continues).
```

### Engine C — in-session self-loop

No machinery restarts you. Crank line: *the worker model itself starts each
next iteration inside its own response; the crank stops the moment the model
ends its message; kill switch: none needed — it dies on its own too easily.*

Continuation discipline:

1. After a Step 6 decision of "continue", the next thing **in the same
   response** is Step 1 of the next iteration. Not a summary, not a question,
   not an offer to continue. A completed iteration is not a completed task.
2. Ending your message while `status` is `"running"` terminates the loop —
   treat it as a bug in your own behavior, equivalent to a driver script
   crashing.
3. You may only end your message when `status` is `converged`, `paused`,
   `blocked`, `stalled`, `aborted`, `max_iterations` — or when the turn genuinely cannot continue
   (context exhaustion), in which case set `status` to `"paused"` first, write
   the journal entry, and tell the user to re-invoke to resume.
4. If a todo tool exists, pre-create one item per remaining budgeted iteration
   and check them off — visible unfinished items counteract the end-turn bias.

Engine C is the least reliable engine by construction. If it keeps dying after
one iteration, recommend the user rerun with Engine B (or A, if attended)
rather than trying harder.

## The per-iteration checklist

Identical for all engines. Run every iteration exactly in this order; do not
skip the recording step even when an iteration "obviously failed".

### Step 1 — Reload state
Read `.loop/LOOP_PLAN.md` and `.loop/state.json`; read `.loop/LESSONS.md` if
present — every sign in it binds this iteration; skim the last 3 journal
entries. Confirm `status` is `running`; if not, print
`LOOP STATUS: <status> — halting` and stop. Disk wins over memory, always.
Operator files: if `.loop/STOP` exists, delete it — then, only if `status`
is `running`, set `aborted`/`manual_abort` (atomic) and halt with the LOOP
STATUS line; a stale STOP beside a terminal status is removed without
overwriting the recorded outcome. If `.loop/STEER.md` exists, it is a
one-shot operator directive — it steers this iteration's hypothesis within
the plan's scope and permissions (it cannot grant new ones), is quoted in
this iteration's journal entry, and is deleted only once that entry is
written (journal-then-delete — a crash before journaling re-applies it).
If the working tree is unexpectedly dirty or the newest record shows a crash
mid-iteration, re-run the cheap verification tier *before* changing anything
and journal the result as the inherited baseline — pre-existing breakage is
attributed to the handoff, never to this iteration's change.

### Step 2 — Plan one change-set
State the single hypothesis this iteration tests: "if I do X, criterion/metric
Y should improve because Z". One hypothesis per iteration — attribution is the
whole point of looping. It must be in scope and within allowed tools; if it
cannot be, go to the escape hatches. Never retry an approach the journal
records as failed unless you can say what is different this time.
If `.loop/backlog.json` exists, the hypothesis targets the highest-priority
item with `"passes": false`. Work discovered mid-iteration becomes a new
backlog item (`"source": "discovered"`) — never a silent extension of this
change-set.

### Step 3 — Execute
Apply the change-set. Track every file touched.

### Step 4 — Verify
Run the plan's verification command(s) **verbatim** and **print the real
output to the conversation** (truncate to the last ~50 lines plus metric lines
for the journal; never paraphrase a result). Two-tier schemes: cheap tier every
iteration, full tier at the plan's cadence and always before declaring
convergence. If the command itself errors (cannot run) twice in a row, that is
the broken-harness hatch — every later "result" would be noise.

### Step 5 — Record (before announcing)
Determine the Step-6 outcome first, then persist it in three places before
announcing or acting on it: (1) the structured record
`.loop/iterations/<NNNN>.json` (schema in the iteration prompt — the
machine-readable source the next agent and the final report recover from);
(2) the journal entry (template below); (3) `state.json` — `iteration` += 1;
`updated_at`; on improvement beyond epsilon or a newly passing criterion,
update `best_metric` and reset `iterations_since_improvement` to 0, else
increment it; `status` and `stop_reason` per the decision. **All JSON writes
are atomic: write `<file>.tmp`, then rename over the target — never edit
`state.json` or a record in place.** Then checkpoint per the git protocol
below. Persisting before announcing means a crash between steps loses
nothing. Backlog runs also update `.loop/backlog.json` atomically here — flip
`passes` (and set `evidence`) only for items this iteration's printed
verification proves; append discovered items. When the journal Note's lesson
is a rule future iterations must obey, also append it to `.loop/LESSONS.md`
as `- SIGN (iter <N>): <rule>`.

### Step 6 — Decide
First match wins:
1. **All criteria pass** (full-tier evidence where defined, printed) →
   `status: "converged"` → Phase 4.
2. **Escape hatch fired** (table below) → corresponding status → Phase 4.
3. **Human checkpoint due** → `status: "paused"`, post checkpoint summary.
4. Otherwise → continue, per your engine's continuation rule.
Always end the iteration with the `LOOP STATUS:` line.

## Exit table — ceilings and escape hatches

Budget and Wall clock are ceilings (designed budget exits); every other row
is an escape hatch (structured early exit). Together with `converged` they
are the complete exit set from the Anatomy section.

| Exit | Condition | Status | stop_reason |
|---|---|---|---|
| Budget | `iteration` ≥ `max_iterations` | `max_iterations` | `iteration_budget_exhausted` |
| Stall | `iterations_since_improvement` ≥ `stall_threshold`, after the plan's stall policy (strategy-switch/rollback) is exhausted | `stalled` | `stalled_no_metric_improvement` |
| Broken harness | verification command errors twice consecutively | `blocked` | `blocked_verification_unavailable` |
| Scope wall | viable fixes require out-of-scope changes or disallowed tools | `blocked` | `blocked_permission_required` |
| Criterion doubt | a criterion appears wrong, self-contradictory, or unachievable | `blocked` | `blocked_criterion_doubt` |
| Checkpoint failure | git/file checkpointing itself fails | `blocked` | `blocked_checkpoint_failed` |
| Wall clock (driver) | `MAX_WALL_MINUTES` reached | `aborted` | `max_wall_time` |
| Dead agent (driver) | two launches without state advance | `blocked` | `stalled_no_state_update` |
| User abort | the user says stop | `aborted` | `manual_abort` |

Budget and stall comparisons use **this iteration's own number** — the value
Step 5 writes (stored `iteration` + 1 at decide time), not the pre-increment
value read at Step 1 — so `max_iterations: 10` means iteration 10 is the last
to run. Engine B's driver enforces the same ceiling pre-launch.

`blocked` always means: stop, explain precisely what blocks, present options.
Never silently work around a block. Under Engine A, remember that any of these
statuses stops the loop only via the condition's STOP clause — and if the
evaluator misses it, the human's `/goal clear` is the real hatch.

### Permission wall protocol

When the scope-wall or permission hatch fires (`blocked` /
`blocked_permission_required`), the pause message **and** the journal Note
must contain exactly these three fields, so the user can unlock the loop in a
single reply:

- **Missing**: the specific permission or path (e.g. `allow_network`,
  `allow_install`, a `deny_paths` entry).
- **Why**: what the current change-set needs it for, concretely.
- **Unlocks**: what granting it enables, and the next intended hypothesis.

On the user's grant, apply it through the amendment protocol (plan §4/§5 +
`.loop/policy.json`, both updated), set `status` back to `"running"` and
`stop_reason` to `null` (atomic write), then resume — under Engine B rerun
`bash .loop/run_loop.sh`; the driver only launches while state says
`running`. Never treat a grant as permanent policy beyond what the user
actually said.

## Journal entry template

Append to `.loop/journal.md`; never rewrite past entries.

```markdown
## Iteration <N> — <ISO8601 timestamp>
**Hypothesis:** <if X then Y because Z>
**Change-set:** <files touched + one-line summary each>
**Verification:** `<command as run>`
```
<verbatim output, last ~50 lines + metric lines>
```
**Criteria:** C1: pass/fail · C2: pass/fail · …
**Metric:** <value or n/a> (best so far: <value>)
**Decision:** continue | converged | paused | <hatch name>
**Note:** <lesson learned; mandatory one-liner if the hypothesis failed>
```

## Git checkpoint protocol (when plan §8 enables git checkpointing)

Before iteration 1 (done during Phase 2 wiring):
- Record the current branch and HEAD sha into `state.json` `git.origin_branch`
  / `git.origin_head` (also used by `check_scope.py` as the diff base).
- The working tree must be clean. If dirty: do not start; set `paused` /
  `user_pause_required` and ask — stashing or committing the user's work is
  their decision, never the loop's.
- Create and switch to `loop/<slug>` from that HEAD. The loop never commits
  outside this branch.

Each iteration (inside Step 5, after the record/journal/state writes):
- Stage the change-set's files (including new in-scope files) and commit with
  message `loop(iter-<N>): <status> — <short summary>`.
- **Commit even when verification failed.** Checkpoints exist for
  recoverability and attribution, not endorsement; pass/fail lives in the
  record and journal.
- Lockfiles / dependency manifests: only if `policy.json`
  `permissions.allow_install` is true AND the paths are not in `deny_paths`.
- If checkpointing itself fails (git errors): `blocked` /
  `blocked_checkpoint_failed`.

End of loop:
- **Never auto-switch back to the origin branch** — an unattended `git
  switch` under the user is a nasty surprise. The final report states origin
  and loop branch; merging or switching back is the user's Phase 4 decision.

No git available → file-copy checkpoints to `.loop/checkpoints/iter-<N>/`;
scope enforcement then falls back to the worker's self-check only (state this
limitation in the plan).

## Amendment protocol

The plan changes mid-run **only** via this path: (1) the user requests it, or
you pause and propose — never amend unilaterally; (2) append to the plan's
`## Amendments` section (`- iter <N>, <timestamp>: <field>: <old> → <new>
(reason)`) and update the field in place — and if the amendment touches plan
§4/§5, **atomically rewrite `.loop/policy.json` to match**: the driver and
`check_scope.py` enforce policy.json, not the plan prose; (3) note it in the current journal
entry; (4) amendments that **loosen completion criteria** require one explicit
confirmation restating what the loosened criterion no longer guarantees.
Quietly lowering the bar is the classic failure mode of long-running loops.
Under Engine A, a criteria amendment also requires the user to re-run `/goal`
with the updated condition — the old condition keeps judging otherwise.

## Resume protocol

On starting with an existing `.loop/` whose status is `running` or `paused`:
read the plan in full, the state, the newest 1-3 records in `.loop/iterations/`, and the journal tail; post a resume
summary (iteration reached, criteria status, best metric, last hypothesis and
outcome, next intended hypothesis); ask resume / restart (archive to
`.loop-archive-<timestamp>/`) / abandon. On resume, continue at Step 1. Under
Engine B this protocol is unnecessary for the invoked iterations (the prompt
handles it) — it applies to interactive sessions rediscovering the loop.

## Conduct rules and why they exist

- **Evidence or it didn't pass.** A criterion counts as passed only when this
  iteration printed verification output demonstrating it. Models under
  iteration pressure drift toward optimistic summaries; verbatim output is the
  antidote — and under Engine A, the evaluator literally cannot see anything
  you don't print.
- **Never weaken the test to pass the test.** Deleting a failing assertion,
  loosening a tolerance, or special-casing the checker is failing the criterion
  plus corrupting the loop. A genuinely wrong test is the criterion-doubt
  hatch: pause and ask.
- **Out-of-scope means out-of-scope**, precisely at the moment touching it
  looks convenient.
- **Do not gold-plate.** Waived concerns are permission to ignore them;
  unrequested improvements consume budget and risk regressions.
- **One iteration, one hypothesis.** Batching five changes destroys
  attribution — you learn nothing from pass or fail. Mid-iteration
  discoveries become backlog items or journal notes, not scope creep.
- **A false status is the one unforgivable move.** Print `LOOP STATUS:
  converged` only when the criteria genuinely passed with evidence printed
  this run. Emitting a status to escape the loop corrupts every downstream
  decision — prefer `blocked` plus an honest reason.
- **Backlog items are append-only.** Flip `passes` with evidence; never
  delete or reword an item — removing a hard item is the checklist version
  of deleting a failing test.
- **User-facing prose in the user's language**; `.loop/` field names, JSON
  keys, statuses, and the `LOOP STATUS:` line stay in English as templated so
  scripts, evaluators, and later sessions can parse them.


